          "source": "def-ck-tensor-field-on-minkowski-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ be the affine Minkowski space\n([[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]]), regarded as the smooth $4$-manifold whose\nsmooth structure is carried by its inertial atlas\n([[lem-affine-minkowski-charts-form-a-smooth-atlas]],\n[[def-smooth-manifold]]), and let $\\Omega\\subseteq A$ be a nonempty open subset.\nLet $r,s\\ge0$ be integers and let $k$ be a nonnegative integer or\n$k=\\infty$.\n\nWrite $T^{r,s}(V)$ for the real vector space of type-$(r,s)$ tensors on $V$,\nthat is, of multilinear maps $(V^{*})^{r}\\times V^{s}\\to\\mathbb R$\n([[def-type-r-s-tensor-on-a-finite-dimensional-vector-space]],\n[[def-algebraic-dual-and-linear-functional]]), and identify\n$T_pA=V$ for every $p\\in A$ through the canonical isomorphism of\n[[lem-tangent-space-of-affine-minkowski-space-is-v]]. A **$\\mathcal C^k$\ntype-$(r,s)$ tensor field on $\\Omega$** is a map $T$ assigning to each\n$p\\in\\Omega$ a tensor $T(p)\\in T^{r,s}(V)$ such that\n$p\\mapsto T(p)$ is of class $\\mathcal C^k$ as a map from the smooth manifold\n$\\Omega$ into the finite-dimensional real vector space $T^{r,s}(V)$\n([[def-c-k-map-between-banach-spaces]]): the space $T^{r,s}(V)$ is a\nfinite-dimensional real vector space, hence a Banach space under any norm\n([[def-norm-and-normed-space]]), and all norms on it are equivalent, so the\nregularity of $p\\mapsto T(p)$ is a property of the map and not of a chosen\nnorm or chart.\n\nThe **components of $T$ in the inertial chart $x$ of a frame**\n$(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ ([[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]) are the\nfunctions\n\n$$T^{a_1\\dots a_r}{}_{b_1\\dots b_s}(p):=T(p)(e^{*a_1},\\dots,e^{*a_r},e_{b_1},\\dots,e_{b_s}),$$\n\nwhere $(e^{*a})$ is the basis of $V^{*}$ dual to $(e_a)$. Then $T$ is\n$\\mathcal C^k$ if and only if all its components in one, equivalently in every,\ninertial chart are $\\mathcal C^k$ functions of $x(p)$: the identification\n$T_pA=V$ of\n[[lem-tangent-space-of-affine-minkowski-space-is-v]] and the linear\nisomorphisms comparing two frames' coordinate expressions are linear, and the\nchart transitions are the affine, hence smooth, maps of\n[[lem-affine-minkowski-charts-form-a-smooth-atlas]], so regularity is\npreserved in both directions; this equivalence is the elementary form of the\ncomponent law proved in [[thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation]]. The\ncomponents are frame-dependent arrays of functions while $T(p)$ is a\nframe-independent multilinear map, and a change of inertial frame changes the\ncomponents by a constant linear transformation of the tensor indices.\n\nA type-$(0,0)$ field is called a **scalar field**, a type-$(1,0)$ field a\n**vector field**, and a type-$(0,1)$ field a **covector field**. For\n$r=s=0$ the definition is that of a $\\mathcal C^k$ real-valued function on\n$\\Omega$.",
